[TEXT] Following the skill's guardrails — named is running (PID 30) and responding (REFUSED received, not timeout), so this is not a listener or firewall issue. The `REFUSED` + "recursion not available" points to a BIND configuration fault. Let me inspect the named.conf directly.
[TOOL] mcp__kathara_base_mcp_server__exec_shell({'host_name': 'srv1', 'command': 'cat /etc/bind/named.conf; echo "====="; cat /etc/bind/named.conf.options 2>/dev/null; echo "====="; ss -ulnp | grep 53; echo "====="; nft list ruleset 2>/dev/null | grep -i "53\\|dns\\|drop"'})
[RESULT] {"result":"// SRV1 campus DNS — minimal authoritative resolver for jeremysitlab.com.\n// Offline lab: no upstream, so recursion is off and the server answers its own\n// zone only (clients/agents that probe 10.5.0.4 get live ARP + DNS responses,\n// so the \"missing DNS server\" baseline gap is gone).\noptions {\n    directory \"/var/cache/bind\";\n    recursion no;\n    allow-query { any; };\n   
[TEXT] **Definitive evidence found.** The `named.conf` on `srv1` explicitly sets `recursion no;`. Named is listening on port 53, no firewall rules block it, but `recursion no;` causes BIND to refuse all recursive queries (RD=1) from every client — which is exactly what all modern resolvers send. The server is only authoritative for `jeremysitlab.com`; any query for names outside that zone (or with RD=1) returns REFUSED.

This affects all hosts in the lab, not just laptop1. I have direct evidence on the owning device. Advancing to Phase 5.

